INT. FIELD HOSPITAL - STADIUM CONCOURSE - NIGHT

A concrete concourse beneath the stands, lined with cots. Overhead, emergency lights flicker. A banner for a long-ago soccer final hangs askew. Medics weave between rows.

DR. ANSELM OYELARAN-PRICE, 50s, in scrubs and a headlamp, bends over a patient. Across from him, DR. KIRSTEN VANTERPOOL, 40s, snaps off a pair of gloves. NURSE BEATRIZ HAMMOND-LU, 30s, holds an IV bag aloft like a torch. A paramedic, JUDE FAIRCLOUGH, 20s, enters pushing an empty gurney.

ANSELM
(to Kirsten)
We lost the monitor.

KIRSTEN
(to Beatriz)
Can you bag him?

BEATRIZ
Already am.

ANSELM
Pressure?

KIRSTEN
Falling.

BEATRIZ
He's gone gray.

ANSELM
Start compressions.

KIRSTEN
I've got them.

ANSELM
On three.

KIRSTEN
One.

ANSELM
Two.

BEATRIZ
Three.

JUDE
(arriving)
What do you need?

ANSELM
Epi, one milligram.

JUDE
Here.

KIRSTEN
Push it.

ANSELM
Pushing.

BEATRIZ
Rhythm?

KIRSTEN
Nothing.

ANSELM
Again.

KIRSTEN
Clear.

JUDE
Clear!

A pause. Then a beep, hesitant, weak. Then another.

BEATRIZ
We've got something.

ANSELM
Pulse?

KIRSTEN
Thready, but there.

ANSELM
Good. Good.

JUDE
Where does he go?

ANSELM
Intensive section. Row four.

KIRSTEN
I'll take him.

ANSELM
No. You'll sit.

KIRSTEN
I'm fine.

ANSELM
You haven't sat in nine hours.

KIRSTEN
Neither have you.

BEATRIZ
Both of you, sit.

ANSELM
We're fine.

KIRSTEN ^
We're fine.

Beatriz glares at them. They sit.
